// orchestration: [topic, module, q, options, answerIdx, explanation, source]
// Verificado contra fuentes oficiales el 2026-10-26 (Kubernetes v1.37). Registro: docs/verificacion/preguntas-orchestration.md
module.exports = [
// ---------- Networking (17) ----------
["Networking", 6, "Which statement reflects the Kubernetes networking model?",
 ["Pods on different nodes must use NAT to communicate", "Every Pod gets its own IP address and can reach all other Pods without NAT", "Each container in a Pod gets a different IP address", "Pods can only communicate with Pods in the same namespace"], [1],
 "El modelo de red de Kubernetes exige una red plana: cada Pod tiene su propia IP y puede comunicarse con cualquier otro Pod sin NAT. Los contenedores de un Pod comparten IP, y el aislamiento por namespace solo se logra con NetworkPolicies.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/#the-kubernetes-network-model"],
["Networking", 6, "Which component is responsible for assigning Pod IP addresses and wiring the Pod network interfaces?",
 ["kube-proxy", "The CNI plugin invoked by the container runtime", "CoreDNS", "kube-scheduler"], [1],
 "Al crear el sandbox del Pod, el runtime invoca el plugin CNI (Calico, Cilium, Flannel, etc.), que crea la interfaz y asigna la IP (IPAM). kube-proxy solo implementa Services y CoreDNS resuelve nombres.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/#the-kubernetes-network-model"],
["Networking", 6, "What is the default Service type when none is specified?",
 ["NodePort", "LoadBalancer", "ClusterIP", "ExternalName"], [2],
 "ClusterIP es el tipo por defecto: asigna una IP virtual accesible solo dentro del clúster. NodePort y LoadBalancer exponen el Service hacia fuera, y ExternalName es un alias DNS.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#publishing-services-service-types"],
["Networking", 6, "What is the default port range used for NodePort Services?",
 ["1-1024", "8000-9000", "30000-32767", "49152-65535"], [2],
 "Por defecto el API server asigna NodePorts en el rango 30000-32767 (configurable con --service-node-port-range). Ese puerto se abre en todos los nodos.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#type-nodeport"],
["Networking", 6, "Which Service type maps a Service to an external DNS name by returning a CNAME record, without any proxying?",
 ["ClusterIP", "ExternalName", "NodePort", "LoadBalancer"], [1],
 "Un Service ExternalName no tiene selector ni IP: el DNS del clúster responde con un CNAME hacia el nombre externo (por ejemplo, una base de datos gestionada) y no se configura ningún proxy.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#externalname"],
["Networking", 6, "How do you create a headless Service?",
 ["Set type: Headless", "Set clusterIP: None", "Omit the ports section", "Set externalTrafficPolicy: Local"], [1],
 "Con clusterIP: None no se asigna IP virtual ni hay balanceo de kube-proxy; el DNS devuelve directamente las IPs de los Pods. Es lo que usan los StatefulSets para nombres estables por Pod. 'Headless' no es un valor válido de type.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#headless-services"],
["Networking", 6, "What is the fully qualified DNS name of a Service named api in the namespace shop, assuming the default cluster domain?",
 ["api.shop.cluster.local", "api.shop.svc.cluster.local", "shop.api.svc.cluster.local", "api.svc.shop.local"], [1],
 "El formato es <service>.<namespace>.svc.<dominio-del-clúster>. Desde el mismo namespace basta con 'api' y desde otro con 'api.shop', gracias a los dominios de búsqueda configurados en el Pod.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/#services"],
["Networking", 6, "Which component provides DNS-based service discovery in most Kubernetes clusters?",
 ["kube-dns-proxy", "CoreDNS", "etcd", "Envoy"], [1],
 "CoreDNS (proyecto graduado de la CNCF) es la implementación por defecto del DNS del clúster; corre en kube-system (Pods con la etiqueta k8s-app=kube-dns, detrás del Service kube-dns) y resuelve nombres de Services y Pods.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/coredns/", "https://www.cncf.io/projects/coredns/"]],
["Networking", 6, "Which resource tracks, in a scalable way, the network endpoints (Pod IPs and ports) that back a Service?",
 ["EndpointSlice", "IngressClass", "NetworkPolicy", "ServicePort"], [0],
 "EndpointSlice divide los endpoints en porciones para escalar a miles de Pods; kube-proxy y otros consumidores la leen. La API v1 Endpoints está obsoleta desde Kubernetes 1.33.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/endpoint-slices/", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#endpoints"]],
["Networking", 6, "What is required for an Ingress resource to have any effect?",
 ["A dedicated LoadBalancer Service per Ingress", "An Ingress controller running in the cluster", "A service mesh", "A special CoreDNS plugin"], [1],
 "El objeto Ingress solo declara reglas HTTP; un controlador (por ejemplo Traefik, HAProxy, Contour o el de un proveedor cloud) debe leerlas y configurar el proxy. Sin controlador el Ingress no hace nada. La API Ingress está congelada y el proyecto recomienda Gateway API.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/#prerequisites"],
["Networking", 6, "Which statement about the Gateway API is correct?",
 ["It is a deprecated alpha API that was replaced by Ingress", "It is a GA, role-oriented API that uses resources such as GatewayClass, Gateway and HTTPRoute", "It only supports raw TCP traffic", "It is built into kube-proxy and needs no controller"], [1],
 "Gateway API (GA desde v1.0, octubre de 2023) es el sucesor de Ingress: se instala como CRDs, separa responsabilidades por rol, es más expresivo (cabeceras, pesos, gRPC, TLS) y, como Ingress, necesita una implementación que la controle.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/gateway/", "https://kubernetes.io/blog/2023/10/31/gateway-api-ga/"]],
["Networking", 6, "Which two Gateway API resources are typically owned by infrastructure providers or cluster operators rather than by application developers? (Choose 2)",
 ["GatewayClass", "Gateway", "HTTPRoute", "Service"], [0, 1],
 "En el modelo de roles, el proveedor de infraestructura define la GatewayClass y el operador del clúster despliega el Gateway (listeners, puertos, TLS). Los desarrolladores gestionan sus HTTPRoute y Services.",
 ["https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/reference/api-types/gatewayclass/", "https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/docs/concepts/api-overview/"]],
["Networking", 6, "The community ingress-nginx controller was retired in 2026. What is the recommended long-term direction for new HTTP routing configurations?",
 ["Expose every application with hostNetwork Pods", "Adopt the Gateway API with a conformant implementation", "Switch all Services to NodePort", "Use ExternalName Services for routing"], [1],
 "El proyecto ingress-nginx de Kubernetes dejó de recibir mantenimiento en marzo de 2026 (sin releases ni parches de seguridad); la comunidad recomienda migrar a Gateway API (o a otro controlador mantenido). Las otras opciones no ofrecen enrutamiento HTTP L7.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/"],
["Networking", 7, "What does a service mesh such as Istio or Linkerd typically provide without changes to application code?",
 ["Container image builds", "Mutual TLS, traffic management, retries and observability between services", "Persistent storage for microservices", "Automatic node provisioning"], [1],
 "Una service mesh añade una capa de infraestructura para la comunicación entre servicios: mTLS automático, enrutamiento, reintentos, timeouts, circuit breaking y métricas/trazas uniformes.",
 ["https://istio.io/latest/about/service-mesh/", "https://linkerd.io/what-is-a-service-mesh/"]],
["Networking", 7, "Traditional service meshes inject a proxy container into every application Pod. What is this pattern called?",
 ["Ambassador node", "Sidecar", "Init container", "DaemonSet gateway"], [1],
 "El proxy sidecar (por ejemplo Envoy o linkerd2-proxy) intercepta el tráfico del Pod. Istio también ofrece el modo ambient, sin sidecars, con un proxy por nodo (ztunnel) y waypoints L7 opcionales.",
 ["https://linkerd.io/what-is-a-service-mesh/", "https://istio.io/latest/docs/overview/dataplane-modes/"]],
["Networking", 6, "Which two Service types expose an application to clients outside the cluster? (Choose 2)",
 ["NodePort", "LoadBalancer", "ClusterIP", "Headless (clusterIP: None)"], [0, 1],
 "NodePort abre un puerto en cada nodo y LoadBalancer provisiona un balanceador externo (que normalmente se apoya en NodePorts). ClusterIP y los Services headless solo son accesibles dentro del clúster.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#publishing-services-service-types"],
["Networking", 6, "Which kube-proxy mode, generally available since Kubernetes 1.33, uses the modern Linux packet-filtering framework as the successor to iptables?",
 ["userspace", "nftables", "ipfw", "kernelspace"], [1],
 "El modo nftables es estable desde 1.33 (requiere kernel 5.13+) y resuelve problemas de rendimiento de iptables, que sigue siendo el modo por defecto en 1.37. userspace ya no existe, ipfw no es un modo de kube-proxy y kernelspace es el modo para nodos Windows; ipvs está obsoleto desde 1.35.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/networking/virtual-ips/#proxy-modes", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/command-line-tools-reference/feature-gates/"]],

// ---------- Security (19) ----------
["Security", 5, "What are the 4C's of cloud native security, from the outermost to the innermost layer?",
 ["Code, Container, Cluster, Cloud", "Cloud, Cluster, Container, Code", "Cluster, Cloud, Code, Container", "Cloud, Container, Cluster, Code"], [1],
 "El modelo de capas es Cloud (o centro de datos), Cluster, Container y Code; cada capa se apoya en la seguridad de la capa exterior. La documentación actual de Kubernetes organiza la seguridad por fases del ciclo de vida, pero el modelo 4C (documentado hasta v1.28) sigue siendo materia de examen.",
 ["https://github.com/kubernetes/website/blob/release-1.28/content/en/docs/concepts/security/overview.md", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/cloud-native-security/"]],
["Security", 5, "Which RBAC object grants permissions within a single namespace?",
 ["ClusterRole", "Role", "ClusterRoleBinding", "ServiceAccount"], [1],
 "Un Role define reglas (verbos sobre recursos) en un namespace concreto. ClusterRole es de ámbito de clúster, ClusterRoleBinding asigna permisos en todo el clúster y una ServiceAccount es una identidad, no un permiso.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/#role-and-clusterrole"],
["Security", 5, "A RoleBinding in the namespace dev references the ClusterRole named view. Which permissions does the subject receive?",
 ["The view permissions in all namespaces", "The view permissions only in the dev namespace", "No permissions, because a RoleBinding cannot reference a ClusterRole", "cluster-admin permissions"], [1],
 "Un RoleBinding puede referenciar un ClusterRole para reutilizar sus reglas, pero los permisos quedan limitados al namespace del RoleBinding. Para todo el clúster se necesitaría un ClusterRoleBinding.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/#rolebinding-and-clusterrolebinding"],
["Security", 5, "Which two statements about Kubernetes RBAC are correct? (Choose 2)",
 ["RBAC permissions are purely additive; there are no deny rules", "A ClusterRoleBinding grants the referenced permissions across the whole cluster", "A Role can contain explicit deny rules", "RBAC rules are defined in NetworkPolicy objects"], [0, 1],
 "RBAC solo concede permisos (todo lo no permitido está denegado) y un ClusterRoleBinding aplica en todos los namespaces. No hay reglas 'deny' y NetworkPolicy controla tráfico de red, no acceso a la API.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/"],
["Security", 5, "Which identity do processes running inside a Pod use to authenticate to the Kubernetes API?",
 ["A User object", "A ServiceAccount", "The Node object", "The kubeconfig of the node"], [1],
 "Cada Pod se ejecuta con una ServiceAccount (default si no se indica) y recibe un token proyectado, de duración limitada y rotación automática, para autenticarse. Kubernetes no tiene objetos User.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/"],
["Security", 5, "How can you check whether your current identity is allowed to delete Deployments in the namespace prod?",
 ["kubectl auth can-i delete deployments -n prod", "kubectl get rolebindings --check", "kubectl describe user me", "kubectl rbac verify deployments"], [0],
 "kubectl auth can-i consulta al API server (SelfSubjectAccessReview) si la acción está permitida; con --as se puede comprobar en nombre de otro usuario o ServiceAccount. Las demás opciones no existen.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/authorization/#checking-api-access"],
["Security", 5, "Which policy levels are defined by the Pod Security Standards?",
 ["Low, Medium and High", "Privileged, Baseline and Restricted", "Permissive, Enforcing and Disabled", "Open, Standard and Hardened"], [1],
 "Privileged no impone restricciones, Baseline evita escaladas de privilegio conocidas y Restricted aplica las mejores prácticas de endurecimiento (no-root, sin capabilities, seccomp, etc.).",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/"],
["Security", 5, "Which namespace label makes Pod Security Admission reject Pods that violate the restricted standard?",
 ["pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted", "security.kubernetes.io/policy: restricted", "psp.kubernetes.io/level: restricted", "admission.k8s.io/restricted: true"], [0],
 "Pod Security Admission se configura con labels en el namespace con el formato pod-security.kubernetes.io/<modo>: <nivel>. Las otras etiquetas no son reconocidas por el controlador.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-admission/#pod-security-admission-labels-for-namespaces"],
["Security", 5, "Besides enforce, which two Pod Security Admission modes exist? (Choose 2)",
 ["audit", "warn", "mutate", "dry-run"], [0, 1],
 "enforce rechaza los Pods que violan el nivel, audit añade una anotación al evento del log de auditoría y warn devuelve una advertencia al usuario. Pod Security Admission valida, no muta Pods.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-admission/#pod-security-levels"],
["Security", 5, "Which built-in mechanism replaced PodSecurityPolicy, which was removed in Kubernetes 1.25?",
 ["NetworkPolicy", "Pod Security Admission", "RuntimeClass", "SecurityContextConstraints"], [1],
 "Pod Security Admission (estable desde 1.25) es el controlador integrado que aplica los Pod Security Standards por namespace; la alternativa es un admission plugin de terceros. SecurityContextConstraints es propio de OpenShift, y NetworkPolicy y RuntimeClass resuelven otros problemas.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-policy/", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-admission/"]],
["Security", 5, "Which securityContext setting prevents a container from running as UID 0?",
 ["privileged: false", "runAsNonRoot: true", "readOnlyRootFilesystem: true", "allowPrivilegeEscalation: true"], [1],
 "Con runAsNonRoot: true el kubelet valida la imagen y se niega a arrancar el contenedor si fuera a ejecutarse como root. readOnlyRootFilesystem protege el sistema de archivos, pero no cambia el usuario.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubernetes-api/workload-resources/pod-v1/#SecurityContext"],
["Security", 5, "Which two securityContext settings help harden a container? (Choose 2)",
 ["allowPrivilegeEscalation: false", "capabilities with drop: ['ALL']", "privileged: true", "runAsUser: 0"], [0, 1],
 "Impedir la escalada de privilegios y eliminar todas las capabilities de Linux reducen la superficie de ataque (ambos son exigidos por el nivel restricted). privileged: true y ejecutar como UID 0 hacen lo contrario.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/#restricted", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/security-context/"]],
["Security", 5, "When no NetworkPolicy selects the Pods in a namespace, how is their traffic treated?",
 ["All ingress and egress traffic is denied", "All traffic is allowed", "Only traffic within the same namespace is allowed", "Only DNS traffic is allowed"], [1],
 "Por defecto Kubernetes permite todo el tráfico entre Pods. Solo cuando una NetworkPolicy selecciona un Pod, ese Pod pasa a aceptar únicamente el tráfico explícitamente permitido.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/#the-two-sorts-of-pod-isolation"],
["Security", 5, "A NetworkPolicy has podSelector: {}, policyTypes: [Ingress] and no ingress rules. What is its effect?",
 ["All Pods in the namespace keep allowing all ingress traffic", "All ingress traffic to every Pod in the namespace is denied", "It applies to Pods in all namespaces", "It has no effect because the podSelector is empty"], [1],
 "Un podSelector vacío selecciona todos los Pods del namespace y, al no haber reglas de ingress, no se permite ninguna entrada: es la política 'default deny ingress'. Las NetworkPolicies son namespaced.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/#default-deny-all-ingress-traffic"],
["Security", 5, "NetworkPolicies have been created, but they have no effect on traffic. What is the most likely reason?",
 ["kube-proxy runs in iptables mode", "The CNI plugin in use does not implement NetworkPolicy", "CoreDNS is not running", "The Pods do not have resource limits"], [1],
 "El API server acepta NetworkPolicies, pero quien las aplica es el plugin de red. CNIs como Calico o Cilium las implementan; otros, como Flannel por sí solo, no.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/#prerequisites"],
["Security", 5, "How can you prevent a Pod from automatically mounting a ServiceAccount token that it does not need?",
 ["Set automountServiceAccountToken: false", "Delete the default ServiceAccount", "Set hostPID: true", "Set imagePullPolicy: Never"], [0],
 "automountServiceAccountToken: false (en el Pod o en la ServiceAccount) evita montar el token, aplicando mínimo privilegio. Borrar la ServiceAccount default no es viable porque el control plane la recrea.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/"],
["Security", 5, "Which CNCF graduated project performs runtime threat detection, for example alerting when a shell is spawned inside a container?",
 ["Falco", "Trivy", "Harbor", "Notary"], [0],
 "Falco (graduado en 2024) analiza las llamadas al sistema del kernel (por defecto con una sonda eBPF moderna) y genera alertas según reglas. Trivy es un escáner de vulnerabilidades, Harbor un registry y Notary firma de contenido; ninguno detecta comportamiento en tiempo de ejecución.",
 ["https://www.cncf.io/projects/falco/", "https://falco.org/docs/"]],
["Security", 5, "Which native, in-process admission mechanism (GA since Kubernetes 1.30) lets administrators write validation rules in CEL without running a webhook?",
 ["ValidatingAdmissionPolicy", "PodSecurityPolicy", "MutatingWebhookConfiguration", "AdmissionReview"], [0],
 "ValidatingAdmissionPolicy evalúa expresiones CEL dentro del API server, evitando la latencia y la operación de un webhook externo. PodSecurityPolicy fue eliminado y AdmissionReview es el formato de mensaje de los webhooks.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/validating-admission-policy/"],
["Security", 5, "What is the purpose of signing container images with tools such as Sigstore cosign?",
 ["To compress image layers", "To verify image provenance and integrity before deployment", "To encrypt image layers at rest", "To speed up image pulls"], [1],
 "La firma permite comprobar quién construyó la imagen y que no fue alterada; políticas de admisión (por ejemplo el policy-controller de Sigstore) pueden exigir firmas válidas. Forma parte de la seguridad de la cadena de suministro junto con SBOMs y escaneo.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/verify-signed-artifacts/"],

// ---------- Troubleshooting (12) ----------
["Troubleshooting", 9, "A node shows STATUS NotReady. What should you check first on that node?",
 ["The kube-scheduler logs", "The kubelet service and its logs, for example with systemctl status kubelet and journalctl -u kubelet", "The CoreDNS configuration", "The Ingress controller"], [1],
 "El estado Ready lo reporta el kubelet; si se detiene, pierde conexión o el runtime/CNI falla, el nodo pasa a NotReady. kubectl describe node muestra las condiciones y los logs del kubelet (journalctl en sistemas con systemd) suelen revelar la causa.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/"],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "A Service exists but requests to it time out, and kubectl get endpointslices shows no endpoints for it. What is the most likely cause?",
 ["The Service type is ClusterIP", "The Service selector does not match the labels of any Ready Pods", "CoreDNS is down", "The Pods have resource limits"], [1],
 "Sin endpoints, kube-proxy no tiene backends a los que enviar tráfico. Suele deberse a labels que no coinciden con el selector o a Pods que no están Ready. Un fallo de DNS daría error de resolución, no timeouts con endpoints vacíos.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-service/#does-the-service-have-any-endpointslices"],
["Troubleshooting", 6, "Applications cannot resolve Service names, but connecting by IP address works. Where should you look first?",
 ["The CoreDNS Pods and their logs in the kube-system namespace", "The etcd database", "The kube-scheduler logs", "The container registry"], [0],
 "Si la conectividad por IP funciona pero la resolución falla, el problema está en el DNS del clúster: Pods de CoreDNS caídos, su Service kube-dns, su ConfigMap o el /etc/resolv.conf del Pod.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/dns-debugging-resolution/"],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "Which command shows the current CPU and memory usage of nodes, and what does it depend on?",
 ["kubectl top nodes, which depends on metrics-server", "kubectl describe nodes, which depends on Prometheus", "kubectl get nodes -o metrics, which depends on kube-state-metrics", "kubectl usage nodes, which depends on the Dashboard"], [0],
 "kubectl top consulta la Metrics API (metrics.k8s.io), que normalmente sirve metrics-server. Sin él, el comando devuelve un error de que la API de métricas no está disponible.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/resource-metrics-pipeline/"],
["Troubleshooting", 8, "A Pod is stuck in Pending and its events say 'pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims'. What should you investigate?",
 ["The image tag", "The PVC, its StorageClass and whether a matching PV or a working provisioner is available", "The liveness probe", "The Service type"], [1],
 "El Pod no puede programarse hasta que su PVC esté Bound. Hay que revisar kubectl describe pvc: StorageClass inexistente, provisionador CSI caído o ningún PV que cumpla tamaño y access mode.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#binding", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/#volume-binding-mode"]],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "A Pod shows ImagePullBackOff. Which two causes are most likely? (Choose 2)",
 ["The image name or tag does not exist in the registry", "Credentials for a private registry are missing or invalid", "The container exceeded its memory limit", "The readiness probe is failing"], [0, 1],
 "ImagePullBackOff indica que el kubelet no pudo descargar la imagen y reintenta con back-off creciente (hasta 5 minutos): nombre o tag erróneo, registry inaccesible o falta de imagePullSecrets. Los problemas de memoria o probes ocurren cuando el contenedor ya existe.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/#imagepullbackoff"],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "A Pod reports CreateContainerConfigError. What is a common cause?",
 ["The node is out of disk space", "A referenced ConfigMap or Secret (or one of its keys) does not exist", "The image is too large", "The Service has no endpoints"], [1],
 "Este error lo devuelve el kubelet cuando no puede generar la configuración del contenedor, típicamente porque una variable de entorno referencia un ConfigMap/Secret o una clave inexistente (sin optional: true). kubectl describe pod muestra el detalle en los eventos.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-pod-configmap/#restrictions", "https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/pkg/kubelet/kuberuntime/kuberuntime_container.go"]],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "In a kubeadm cluster the kube-apiserver is not responding. How is it usually deployed, which tells you where to look?",
 ["As a Deployment in the default namespace", "As a static Pod whose manifest lives in /etc/kubernetes/manifests on the control plane node", "As a systemd service on the worker nodes", "As a DaemonSet running on every node"], [1],
 "kubeadm ejecuta el API server, el scheduler, el controller-manager y etcd como static Pods gestionados por el kubelet. Si el API server cae, se revisa el manifiesto, los logs del contenedor (crictl logs) y los del kubelet.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/setup-tools/kubeadm/implementation-details/", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/static-pod/"]],
["Troubleshooting", 5, "Two Pods in different namespaces can no longer communicate after a security change, while DNS resolution still works. What is the most likely cause?",
 ["A NetworkPolicy that does not allow that traffic", "An incorrect imagePullPolicy", "A missing PriorityClass", "A LimitRange in one of the namespaces"], [0],
 "Tras introducir NetworkPolicies, el tráfico no permitido explícitamente se bloquea. Hay que revisar las políticas de ingress del destino y de egress del origen (incluyendo namespaceSelector).",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"],
["Troubleshooting", 4, "A Pod is stuck in Pending. Which two causes are plausible? (Choose 2)",
 ["No node has enough allocatable CPU or memory for the Pod's requests", "The Pod does not tolerate the taints present on the available nodes", "The application inside the container throws exceptions at startup", "The liveness probe is misconfigured"], [0, 1],
 "Pending significa que el Pod aún no se ha programado (o no se han creado sus contenedores): falta de recursos, taints sin toleration, afinidad imposible o PVC sin enlazar. Las excepciones y probes solo afectan a contenedores ya en ejecución.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/#pod-phase", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/"]],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "A container terminated with exit code 137. What does that usually indicate?",
 ["Successful completion", "It was killed with SIGKILL, frequently because it ran out of memory (OOMKilled)", "The command was not found in the image", "A DNS lookup failed"], [1],
 "137 = 128 + 9 (SIGKILL). Suele deberse a OOMKilled por superar el límite de memoria: el estado lastState.terminated muestra exitCode 137 y reason OOMKilled. El código 0 indica éxito.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/assign-memory-resource/#exceed-a-container-s-memory-limit"],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "Pods on a node are being evicted and the node condition shows DiskPressure=True. What is happening?",
 ["The node is low on available disk space or inodes and the kubelet is reclaiming resources", "The scheduler is rebalancing Pods across nodes", "A NetworkPolicy is blocking the node", "etcd has run out of space"], [0],
 "Cuando el espacio o los inodos caen por debajo de los umbrales de desalojo, el kubelet marca DiskPressure, elimina Pods/contenedores muertos e imágenes sin uso y, si no basta, desaloja Pods.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/node-pressure-eviction/#node-conditions"],

// ---------- Storage (14) ----------
["Storage", 8, "What is the lifetime of an emptyDir volume?",
 ["It persists after the Pod is deleted", "It exists while the Pod runs on that node and is deleted when the Pod is removed", "It is shared by all Pods in the namespace", "It is always backed by a cloud disk"], [1],
 "emptyDir se crea vacío al asignar el Pod a un nodo, sobrevive a reinicios de contenedores y se borra al eliminar el Pod. Sirve para caché o para compartir archivos entre contenedores del mismo Pod.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#emptydir"],
["Storage", 8, "Which object represents a user's request for storage, specifying size and access mode?",
 ["PersistentVolume", "PersistentVolumeClaim", "StorageClass", "VolumeAttachment"], [1],
 "El PVC es la solicitud que hace el usuario; se enlaza a un PV que cumpla los requisitos (o provoca su aprovisionamiento dinámico). El PV es el recurso de almacenamiento en sí.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#introduction"],
["Storage", 8, "Which object enables dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes by naming a provisioner and its parameters?",
 ["StorageClass", "PersistentVolumeClaim", "CSIDriver", "ConfigMap"], [0],
 "La StorageClass define el provisionador (normalmente un driver CSI), parámetros como el tipo de disco, la reclaimPolicy y el volumeBindingMode. Un PVC que la referencia provoca la creación automática del PV.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/"],
["Storage", 8, "Which access mode allows a volume to be mounted read-write by many nodes at the same time?",
 ["ReadWriteOnce", "ReadOnlyMany", "ReadWriteMany", "ReadWriteOncePod"], [2],
 "ReadWriteMany (RWX) permite lectura y escritura desde múltiples nodos; requiere un backend que lo soporte, como NFS o CephFS. ReadOnlyMany solo permite lectura.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#access-modes"],
["Storage", 8, "Which access mode guarantees that only a single Pod in the whole cluster can read and write the volume?",
 ["ReadWriteOnce", "ReadWriteOncePod", "ReadOnlyMany", "ReadWriteMany"], [1],
 "ReadWriteOncePod (GA desde 1.29, solo volúmenes CSI) restringe el acceso a un único Pod. ReadWriteOnce restringe a un nodo, por lo que varios Pods en ese nodo podrían montarlo.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#access-modes"],
["Storage", 8, "With persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain, what happens when the bound PVC is deleted?",
 ["The PV and its data are deleted", "The PV and its data are kept; the PV becomes Released and needs manual cleanup before reuse", "The PV is automatically bound to a new PVC", "The data is wiped and the PV becomes Available"], [1],
 "Retain conserva el volumen y los datos para recuperación manual; el PV queda Released y no se reutiliza automáticamente. Delete borraría el PV y el disco subyacente.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#retain"],
["Storage", 8, "What is the default reclaim policy for dynamically provisioned PersistentVolumes?",
 ["Retain", "Recycle", "Delete", "Archive"], [2],
 "Los PV dinámicos heredan la reclaimPolicy de la StorageClass, cuyo valor por defecto es Delete. Recycle está obsoleto y Archive no existe.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#reclaim-policy", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/#reclaim-policy"]],
["Storage", 8, "What does volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer in a StorageClass do?",
 ["It delays provisioning and binding until a Pod that uses the PVC is scheduled, so topology constraints are respected", "It waits for an administrator to approve the PVC", "It mounts the volume read-only first", "It deletes the volume after the first Pod finishes"], [0],
 "Con WaitForFirstConsumer el volumen se crea en la zona del nodo donde se programa el Pod, evitando discos creados en una zona distinta. El modo Immediate (por defecto) aprovisiona en cuanto se crea el PVC.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/#volume-binding-mode"],
["Storage", 8, "Which standard interface allows storage vendors to write plugins that work with Kubernetes without changing Kubernetes core code?",
 ["CRI", "CNI", "CSI", "SMI"], [2],
 "La Container Storage Interface sustituyó a los plugins 'in-tree'; los proveedores entregan drivers CSI independientes. CRI es para runtimes, CNI para red y SMI fue una especificación de service mesh.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#csi"],
["Storage", 8, "How does a StatefulSet give each replica its own PersistentVolumeClaim?",
 ["Using volumeClaimTemplates", "Using a shared hostPath volume", "Using an emptyDir per Pod", "Using a ConfigMap per replica"], [0],
 "volumeClaimTemplates genera un PVC por réplica (por ejemplo data-db-0, data-db-1) que se conserva aunque el Pod se reprograme, manteniendo su identidad y sus datos.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#volume-claim-templates"],
["Storage", 8, "A PVC remains in Pending status. Which is a likely reason?",
 ["No PV matches its size or access mode and no StorageClass can provision one", "The Pod has no readiness probe", "The Service is headless", "The node is cordoned"], [0],
 "Un PVC queda Pending si no hay PV compatible ni aprovisionamiento dinámico posible (StorageClass inexistente o provisionador fallando). Con WaitForFirstConsumer también queda Pending hasta que un Pod lo use, lo cual es normal.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#binding"],
["Storage", 8, "Why is hostPath generally discouraged for application workloads?",
 ["It is slower than emptyDir", "It exposes the node's filesystem to the Pod, creating security risks and tying data to a single node", "It requires a CSI driver", "It cannot be mounted read-only"], [1],
 "hostPath monta rutas del nodo dentro del Pod: puede permitir escapar al host y los datos quedan atados a ese nodo. El nivel baseline de Pod Security lo prohíbe.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#hostpath", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/#baseline"]],
["Storage", 8, "Which two statements about PersistentVolumes and PersistentVolumeClaims are correct? (Choose 2)",
 ["A PVC is a namespaced object", "A PV is a cluster-scoped object", "A PV can be bound to several PVCs at the same time", "Only cluster administrators can create PVCs"], [0, 1],
 "Los PVC viven en el namespace de la aplicación y los PV son recursos del clúster. La relación PV-PVC es uno a uno, y cualquier usuario con permisos RBAC puede crear PVCs.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/"],
["Storage", 8, "Which StorageClass field must be enabled so that users can grow a PVC by editing its requested size?",
 ["allowVolumeExpansion: true", "reclaimPolicy: Retain", "volumeBindingMode: Immediate", "mountOptions: resize"], [0],
 "Con allowVolumeExpansion: true se puede aumentar spec.resources.requests.storage del PVC y el driver CSI amplía el volumen. Reducir el tamaño no está soportado.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#expanding-persistent-volumes-claims"],

// ---------- Añadidas 2026-10-26: módulo 07 (service mesh y microservicios) ----------
["Networking", 7, "Which is a key advantage of a microservices architecture compared with a monolith?",
 ["Each service can be deployed, updated and scaled independently", "There are fewer components to deploy and operate", "All services must share the same language and release cycle", "Network communication between components is eliminated"], [0],
 "Los microservicios dividen la aplicación en servicios independientes que se despliegan y escalan por separado. A cambio aumentan los componentes y la comunicación por red, la complejidad operativa que un service mesh ayuda a gestionar.",
 "https://glossary.cncf.io/microservices-architecture/"],
["Networking", 7, "How is a native sidecar container (stable since Kubernetes 1.33) declared in a Pod spec?",
 ["As an entry in initContainers with restartPolicy: Always", "As a regular container with the annotation sidecar: \"true\"", "As an ephemeral container added with kubectl debug", "As a separate Pod placed on the same node with podAffinity"], [0],
 "Un native sidecar es un init container con restartPolicy: Always: arranca antes que los contenedores de la app, sigue corriendo toda la vida del Pod, se detiene después de ellos y no impide que un Job se complete.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/sidecar-containers/"],
["Networking", 7, "What is Envoy, as used by service meshes such as Istio?",
 ["A high-performance L7 proxy that commonly acts as the mesh data plane", "The control plane component that issues mTLS certificates", "A container runtime that implements the CRI", "A CNI plugin that assigns Pod IP addresses"], [0],
 "Envoy (proyecto graduado de la CNCF) es un proxy L7 que corre fuera del proceso de la aplicación y recibe su configuración dinámicamente desde un control plane. Istio lo usa como sidecar y en los waypoints.",
 ["https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/what_is_envoy", "https://www.cncf.io/projects/envoy/"]],
["Networking", 7, "In Istio, which component belongs to the control plane rather than the data plane?",
 ["istiod, which distributes proxy configuration and issues mTLS certificates", "The Envoy sidecar proxy running in each Pod", "The ztunnel proxy running on each node", "A waypoint proxy deployed for L7 traffic"], [0],
 "El data plane son los proxies que tocan el tráfico (sidecars Envoy, ztunnel, waypoints). istiod es el control plane: descubrimiento de servicios, distribución de configuración y CA de certificados para mTLS.",
 ["https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/deployment/architecture/", "https://istio.io/latest/docs/ambient/overview/"]],
["Networking", 7, "Which two traffic-management features can a service mesh apply between services without changing application code? (Choose 2)",
 ["Automatic retries and request timeouts", "Weighted traffic splitting for canary releases", "Building and signing container images", "Dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes"], [0, 1],
 "La mesh configura en los proxies reintentos, timeouts, circuit breaking y reparto de tráfico por pesos (canary, A/B) sin tocar el código. Construir imágenes o aprovisionar volúmenes no es función de una service mesh.",
 "https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/traffic-management/"],
["Networking", 7, "In Istio ambient mode, what provides L4 features such as mTLS to workloads without sidecars?",
 ["ztunnel, a purpose-built per-node proxy", "A waypoint proxy injected into every Pod", "kube-proxy running in nftables mode", "CoreDNS"], [0],
 "En ambient no hay sidecars: ztunnel, un proxy por nodo, aporta mTLS, identidad y telemetría L4. Los waypoint proxies (Envoy) son opcionales, no se inyectan en cada Pod y solo se despliegan cuando hacen falta funciones L7.",
 ["https://istio.io/latest/docs/ambient/overview/", "https://istio.io/latest/docs/overview/dataplane-modes/"]],
["Networking", 7, "Which statement about Linkerd is correct?",
 ["It is a CNCF graduated service mesh whose data plane is a lightweight micro-proxy written in Rust", "It uses Envoy as its sidecar proxy", "It is a CNCF sandbox Ingress controller", "It is a container runtime that replaces containerd"], [0],
 "Linkerd (graduado en la CNCF en 2021) usa linkerd2-proxy, un micro-proxy en Rust inyectado como sidecar, no Envoy. Su control plane emite los certificados para el mTLS entre proxies.",
 ["https://linkerd.io/2/reference/architecture/", "https://www.cncf.io/projects/linkerd/"]],
["Networking", 7, "Following the Gateway API GAMMA initiative, how is an HTTPRoute attached to manage east-west (mesh) traffic for a Service?",
 ["Its parentRefs point to the Service instead of a Gateway", "It must reference a GatewayClass named mesh", "It is converted into an Ingress object by the mesh", "It is attached to a NetworkPolicy"], [0],
 "En GAMMA las rutas (HTTPRoute, GRPCRoute) se asocian directamente a un Service mediante parentRefs y aplican al tráfico dirigido a él; en mesh no se suelen usar Gateway ni GatewayClass. El soporte de mesh es GA desde Gateway API v1.1.",
 ["https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/docs/mesh/", "https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/docs/mesh/gamma/"]],

// ---------- Añadidas 2026-10-26: troubleshooting del clúster (módulo 09) ----------
["Troubleshooting", 9, "kubectl describe node shows the Ready condition as Unknown with the message 'Kubelet stopped posting node status'. What does this indicate?",
 ["The control plane is no longer receiving status updates from that node's kubelet", "The node has run out of disk space", "The node was cordoned for maintenance", "CoreDNS cannot resolve the node's hostname"], [0],
 "El kubelet informa periódicamente del estado del nodo; si deja de hacerlo (nodo apagado, kubelet caído o problema de red), la condición Ready pasa a Unknown y el nodo aparece NotReady. La falta de disco se refleja como DiskPressure.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/#example-debugging-a-down-unreachable-node"],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "A worker node has become unreachable and shows NotReady. What happens to the Pods running on it, by default?",
 ["The node is tainted as unreachable and its Pods are evicted after about five minutes", "The node object is deleted immediately", "The control plane restarts the kubelet remotely", "The running containers are live-migrated to another node"], [0],
 "El control plane añade los taints node.kubernetes.io/unreachable (NoSchedule y NoExecute) y, por defecto, los Pods se desalojan tras unos cinco minutos para que sus controladores los recreen en otros nodos. Kubernetes no migra contenedores en caliente.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/#example-debugging-a-down-unreachable-node"],
["Troubleshooting", 9, "The kube-apiserver of a cluster crashes. What happens to the Pods that are already running on the worker nodes?",
 ["They keep running, but Pods cannot be created, updated or stopped through the API until it recovers", "All Pods are terminated immediately", "All Pods are rescheduled onto the control plane nodes", "The Pods are paused until etcd is restored from a backup"], [0],
 "Si cae el API server no se pueden crear, actualizar ni detener Pods, pero los Pods y Services existentes siguen funcionando salvo que dependan de la API. Por eso se recomienda un control plane en alta disponibilidad.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/#cluster-failure-modes"],
// ---------- Añadidas 2026-10-29: Storage (+14, ver docs/verificacion/preguntas-ampliacion-2026-10-29.md) ----------
["Storage", 8, "A Pod uses an emptyDir volume with medium: Memory as a fast scratch cache. What should you keep in mind?",
 ["Files written to it count against the memory limit of the container that wrote them", "Its contents survive Pod deletion because they are kept in node RAM", "It is shared with every other Pod scheduled on the same node", "It requires a CSI driver that supports RAM-backed volumes"], [0],
 "Con medium: Memory, Kubernetes monta un tmpfs (sistema de archivos en RAM): es muy rápido, pero lo que se escribe cuenta para el límite de memoria del contenedor que lo escribió. Sigue siendo un emptyDir: vive lo que vive el Pod y no se comparte con otros Pods.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#emptydir"],
["Storage", 8, "A Pod defines a generic ephemeral volume (spec.volumes[].ephemeral.volumeClaimTemplate). What happens to the PVC created for it when the Pod is deleted?",
 ["The PVC is owned by the Pod and is garbage-collected together with it", "The PVC is kept so that a new Pod with the same name can reuse it", "The PVC becomes Released and waits for an administrator", "The PVC is converted into an emptyDir volume on the node"], [0],
 "El Pod es el dueño (owner) del PVC que se crea para el volumen efímero genérico (con nombre <pod>-<volumen>). Al borrar el Pod, el recolector de basura borra el PVC y, con la reclaimPolicy Delete por defecto, también el volumen. Conservar el PVC para un Pod con el mismo nombre es lo que hacen los volumeClaimTemplates de un StatefulSet.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/ephemeral-volumes/#lifecycle-and-persistentvolumeclaim"],
["Storage", 8, "A container mounts a single file from a ConfigMap using subPath. Later the ConfigMap is edited. What does the running container see?",
 ["The old content, because subPath mounts do not receive ConfigMap updates", "The new content after the next kubelet sync, like any ConfigMap volume", "The new content immediately, because subPath mounts are live links", "An empty file until the kubelet restarts the container"], [0],
 "Un ConfigMap montado como volumen completo se actualiza solo (eventualmente, en el sync periódico del kubelet). Un montaje con subPath, en cambio, no recibe actualizaciones: hay que recrear el Pod para ver el cambio.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/#mounted-configmaps-are-updated-automatically", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#configmap"]],
["Storage", 8, "A PVC sets storageClassName: \"\" in a cluster that has a default StorageClass. How is it handled?",
 ["It can bind only to PVs with no class, so dynamic provisioning is disabled for it", "It is assigned the default StorageClass and provisioned dynamically", "It binds to any available PV regardless of the PV's class", "The API server rejects it because the class name is empty"], [0],
 "storageClassName: \"\" pide explícitamente un PV sin clase: solo se enlaza con PVs sin clase y no dispara aprovisionamiento dinámico. La StorageClass por defecto se usa cuando se omite el campo, no cuando se deja vacío.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#class-1", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/#default-storageclass"]],
["Storage", 8, "Two Pods scheduled on the same node both mount the same PVC, whose access mode is ReadWriteOnce. What happens?",
 ["Both Pods can read and write it, because RWO limits access to one node, not one Pod", "Only the first Pod mounts it and the second stays in ContainerCreating", "The second Pod automatically gets the volume mounted read-only", "The scheduler moves the second Pod to a different node"], [0],
 "ReadWriteOnce permite montar el volumen en lectura-escritura desde un único nodo, y varios Pods de ese nodo pueden usarlo a la vez. Para limitar el acceso a un solo Pod en todo el clúster existe ReadWriteOncePod.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#access-modes"],
["Storage", 8, "A 20Gi PVC from a StorageClass with allowVolumeExpansion: true is edited to request 10Gi. What happens?",
 ["The change is not accepted: Kubernetes does not support shrinking a PVC below its current size", "The volume is shrunk and its file system is resized online", "The PV is deleted and a new 10Gi volume is provisioned", "The PVC is shrunk the next time its Pod is restarted"], [0],
 "La expansión solo funciona hacia arriba: Kubernetes no permite reducir un PVC por debajo de su capacidad actual (status.capacity). Si se necesita un volumen más pequeño, hay que crear un PVC nuevo.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#recovering-from-failure-when-expanding-volumes"],
["Storage", 8, "You want a StatefulSet to delete the PVCs of the replicas removed when you scale it down, but keep a PVC when its Pod is merely recreated. What do you configure?",
 ["persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy with whenScaled: Delete", "reclaimPolicy: Delete in the StorageClass", "podManagementPolicy: Parallel in the StatefulSet", "persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Delete on each PV"], [0],
 "persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy (estable desde v1.32) tiene dos campos, whenScaled y whenDeleted, cada uno con valor Retain (defecto) o Delete. Con whenScaled: Delete solo se borran los PVCs de las réplicas que se eliminan al reducir. La reclaimPolicy decide qué pasa con el PV cuando se borra el PVC, pero no borra el PVC.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#persistentvolumeclaim-retention"],
["Storage", 8, "You have a VolumeSnapshot of a database volume and need a new volume pre-populated with that data. What do you create?",
 ["A new PVC whose dataSource references the VolumeSnapshot", "A new VolumeSnapshotClass that points to the original PV", "A PV with persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Recycle", "A VolumeSnapshotContent inside the Pod's namespace"], [0],
 "Para restaurar se crea un PVC nuevo con dataSource (kind: VolumeSnapshot), y el driver CSI aprovisiona un volumen con los datos del snapshot. VolumeSnapshotContent es el snapshot real y es un recurso del clúster, como un PV, sin namespace; no es el mecanismo de restauración.",
 ["https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/#provisioning-volumes-from-snapshots", "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#create-persistent-volume-claim-from-volume-snapshot"]],
["Storage", 8, "A Pod in namespace 'web' sets persistentVolumeClaim.claimName: data, but the PVC 'data' exists only in namespace 'db'. What is true?",
 ["It cannot work: a claim must exist in the same namespace as the Pod using it", "It works, because PVCs are cluster-scoped just like PVs", "It works as long as the PV's reclaim policy is Retain", "It works if the Pod sets the PVC namespace in volumeMounts"], [0],
 "Los PVC son objetos con namespace y un Pod solo puede usar claims de su propio namespace. Los que no tienen namespace (cluster-scoped) son los PV, no los PVC.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#claims-as-volumes"],
["Storage", 8, "Which two volume sources can be combined into a single directory with a projected volume? (Choose 2)",
 ["secret", "serviceAccountToken", "persistentVolumeClaim", "hostPath"], [0, 1],
 "Un volumen projected combina en un mismo directorio fuentes como secret, configMap, downwardAPI y serviceAccountToken; así se monta el token de la ServiceAccount en los Pods. persistentVolumeClaim y hostPath no son fuentes que se puedan proyectar.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/projected-volumes/#introduction"],
["Storage", 8, "A CSI driver's node plugin must run on every node where Pods may mount its volumes. Which workload type is typically used to deploy it?",
 ["DaemonSet", "Job", "CronJob", "A single-replica ReplicaSet"], [0],
 "Según la documentación de Kubernetes CSI, el componente de nodo se despliega en todos los nodos mediante un DaemonSet. El plugin de controlador, en cambio, puede ir en un Deployment o un StatefulSet en cualquier nodo.",
 "https://kubernetes-csi.github.io/docs/deploying.html#node-plugin"],
["Storage", 8, "A PVC requests 100Gi ReadWriteOnce. The only Available PVs of its class are a 50Gi RWO PV and a 150Gi RWO PV, and the class has no dynamic provisioner. What happens?",
 ["The PVC binds to the 150Gi PV and gets the whole volume", "The PVC binds to the 50Gi PV and the volume is expanded", "The 150Gi PV is split and 100Gi is given to this PVC", "The PVC stays Pending because no PV matches exactly"], [0],
 "El binding PV-PVC es 1:1 y el usuario siempre obtiene al menos lo que pidió, aunque el volumen puede ser mayor. Un PV más pequeño nunca sirve, y un PV no se reparte entre varios PVCs.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#binding"],
["Storage", 8, "A user deletes a PVC that is still mounted by a running Pod. What happens?",
 ["The PVC shows Terminating and is removed only when no Pod uses it", "The PVC is deleted at once and the Pod loses access to its data", "The API server rejects the delete request with an error", "The Pod is evicted immediately so that the PVC can be deleted"], [0],
 "Storage Object in Use Protection añade el finalizer kubernetes.io/pvc-protection: el PVC queda en Terminating y su eliminación se pospone hasta que ningún Pod lo use activamente. La petición de borrado sí se acepta; lo que se retrasa es que se complete.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#storage-object-in-use-protection"],
["Storage", 8, "Where does the kubelet keep the files of a Secret volume mounted into a Pod?",
 ["In tmpfs (RAM-backed), so they are never written to the node's non-volatile storage", "In a directory on the node's root disk under /var/lib", "Inside the writable layer of the container image", "In etcd, read remotely on every file access"], [0],
 "Los volúmenes secret usan tmpfs (sistema de archivos en RAM), así que su contenido nunca se escribe en el almacenamiento persistente del nodo. Además se montan siempre de solo lectura.",
 "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#secret"]
];
